module rnaYieldDetection
module rnaPurityDetection

manifest WholeBlood
manifest LysisSolution
manifest RNABindingBeads
manifest Wash1Solution
manifest Wash2Solution
manifest ElutionBuffer
manifest FluorinertFC40
manifest PluronicF127

// ============================================================
// World-to-Digital-Microfluidic Interface Enabling Extraction
// and Purification of RNA from Human Whole Blood.
// Jebrail et al., Anal. Chem. 2014, 86, 3856-3862.
// DOI: 10.1021/ac404085p
//
// System overview:
//   The world-to-DMF system bridges macroscale sample handling
//   (Extraction Module) with microscale DMF purification
//   (Purification Module) via a 10 uL Bridging Droplet formed
//   on the Bridging Electrode at the module interface.
//
//   Extraction Module: two 500 uL microcentrifuge tubes
//     (Reaction Chamber and Waste Chamber) driven by a single
//     peristaltic pump via Connector Tubes and Transfer Tubes.
//   Purification Module: DMF device with 40 chromium electrode
//     pads (6x6 mm, 40 um interelectrode gaps), Parylene-C
//     dielectric (7 um), Teflon-AF hydrophobic coating (50 nm),
//     300 um air gap. Droplets actuated at AC 100 Vrms.
//   Bridging Electrode: exposed ~1 mm x 6 mm edge of the bottom
//     plate, partially uncovered by the top plate, accepting
//     Transfer Tubes at ~70 degrees angle.
//   Bridging Droplet: 10 uL droplet formed on the Bridging
//     Electrode. The large-volume lysate is routed through
//     this droplet three times while a magnet beneath immobilises
//     RNA-bound magnetic beads; supernatant passes to waste.
//
// Blood specimen volumes: 25, 50, or 100 uL (fingerstick range).
//   This file encodes the 50 uL blood specimen arm as the
//   representative protocol (intermediate volume).
//   Lysis Solution: 130 uL; RNA Binding Beads: 20 uL;
//   total mixture volume driven through Bridging Droplet: 200 uL.
//
// Processing performance vs. conventional method:
//   >2-fold faster (20 min vs 45 min).
//   12-fold less reagent consumption (microscale 5-15 uL vs
//   macroscale 150-300 uL wash volumes).
//   RNA yields and quality (A260/A280, RIN) fully comparable.
//   Supports downstream qRT-PCR and RNA-Seq analyses.
//
// NOTE ON BRIDGING DROPLET ABSTRACTION:
//   The core world-to-DMF transition -- driving the macroscale
//   lysate through the 10 uL Bridging Droplet via peristaltic
//   pump while the magnet below captures bead-bound RNA -- is
//   modelled here as repeated `mix` operations. In the physical
//   system this is a pressure-driven convective flow event
//   (pump aspirates into Transfer Tube 2 while dispensing from
//   Transfer Tube 1), not a DMF electrode mixing step. The
//   convective transport is responsible for the slightly higher
//   RNA capture efficiency observed at 100 uL blood volume vs
//   the conventional diffusion-limited method.
//
// NOTE ON MAGNET STEPS:
//   Magnet engagement (bead immobilisation) and disengagement
//   (bead resuspension) are modelled as `heat` statements at
//   25 C (room temperature) for the duration of the operation,
//   as BioScript has no native magnet instruction. This is
//   consistent with the approach used in prior BioScript
//   encodings of magnetic bead protocols.
//
// NOTE ON SURFACTANT ADDITIVES:
//   Pluronic F127 (0.1% w/v) is added to the Elution Buffer
//   per the MagMAX kit adaptation (ref 20 in paper) to maintain
//   droplet actuation fidelity on the Teflon-AF surface.
//   FC-40 (Fluorinert) is the filler fluid in the DMF air gap.
//   Both are modelled as manifest reagents; their dispensing
//   into the device prior to the protocol begins is abstracted
//   in the device initialisation block below.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main            -- device init; lysis; bead binding;
//                          world-to-DMF transition (Bridging
//                          Droplet formation and bead capture);
//                          Wash 1; Wash 2; Elution
//   BB2  yieldPass       -- yieldSignal > 0: acceptable RNA yield;
//                          dispose eluate, proceed to purity QC
//   BB3  yieldFail       -- yieldSignal <= 0: insufficient yield;
//                          drain eluate
//   BB4  yieldJoin       -- join: purity QC branch begins
//   BB5  purityPass      -- puritySignal > 0: A260/A280 >= 1.8;
//                          dispose; experiment complete
//   BB6  purityFail      -- puritySignal <= 0: low purity; drain
//   BB7  finalCleanup    -- terminal: dispose QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// DEVICE INITIALISATION:
//   Load filler fluid (FC-40 + Pluronic F127) into the DMF
//   air gap between bottom and top plates to enable droplet
//   actuation. Preload Purification Module reservoirs:
//     Wash 1 Solution:  15 uL (one unit)
//     Wash 2 Solution:  15 uL (one unit)
//     Elution Buffer:   10 uL (one unit, supplemented with
//                              0.1% w/v Pluronic F127)
//   Adjust magnetic stand so Transfer Tubes contact Bridging
//   Electrode at ~70 degrees. DMF actuation: AC 100 Vrms.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

fillerFluid      = dispense 1 units of FluorinertFC40
surfactant       = dispense 1 units of PluronicF127
devicePrime      = mix fillerFluid with surfactant for 30s

// Preload wash and elution reagents into on-device reservoirs.
// These droplets sit at rest until actuated by electrowetting.
wash1Reservoir   = dispense 1 units of Wash1Solution
wash2Reservoir   = dispense 1 units of Wash2Solution
elutionReservoir = dispense 1 units of ElutionBuffer

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Introduce blood specimen into Reaction Chamber.
//   50 uL whole blood pipetted directly into Reaction Chamber
//   (500 uL microcentrifuge tube).
//   Blood specimens from healthy donors (ProMedDx, Norton MA).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bloodSpecimen    = dispense 1 units of WholeBlood

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Aspirate Lysis Solution from Transfer Tube 1 into
//   Reaction Chamber and actively mix with blood specimen.
//   Lysis Solution: 130 uL (for 50 uL blood; 2.6:1 v/v ratio).
//   Mixing: shuttle bolus between Transfer Tube 1 and Reaction
//   Chamber for 30 s via peristaltic pump.
//   MagMAX-Blood kit lysis buffer (Life Technologies).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lysisSolution    = dispense 1 units of LysisSolution
bloodLysate      = mix bloodSpecimen with lysisSolution for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Aspirate RNA Binding Beads from Transfer Tube 1
//   into Reaction Chamber. Mix with lysate for 30 s, then
//   allow bead-RNA binding to proceed for 5 min (300 s).
//   Bead volume: 20 uL. MagMAX silica-coated magnetic beads.
//   Total reaction volume: 200 uL (50 + 130 + 20 uL).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

rnaBeads         = dispense 1 units of RNABindingBeads
beadLysateMix    = mix bloodLysate with rnaBeads for 30s

// 5 min bead-RNA binding incubation at room temperature (300 s).
heat beadLysateMix at 25c for 300s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Form Bridging Droplet and execute world-to-DMF
//   transition (Pass 1 of 3).
//   The peristaltic pump drives the full 200 uL reaction
//   mixture from Transfer Tube 1 through the 10 uL Bridging
//   Droplet on the Bridging Electrode and into Transfer Tube 2.
//   The Bridging Electrode is activated (AC 100 Vrms) to pin
//   the droplet and prevent spread to adjacent electrodes.
//   A movable external magnet is positioned directly beneath
//   the Bridging Electrode to immobilise bead-bound RNA as
//   the lysate flows through.
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts pressure-driven convective transport
//   through the Bridging Droplet, not DMF electrode mixing.
//   Pass 1: establish Bridging Droplet and begin bead capture.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bridgingDroplet1 = mix beadLysateMix with devicePrime for 5s

// Engage magnet beneath Bridging Electrode to capture beads
// (modelled as heat at 25 C for duration of flow-through).
heat bridgingDroplet1 at 25c for 60s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: World-to-DMF transition -- Pass 2 of 3.
//   Pump shuttles lysate back through Bridging Droplet a second
//   time (Transfer Tube 2 -> Bridging Droplet -> Transfer Tube 1)
//   with magnet engaged to maximise bead recovery from the
//   macroscale volume. Pressure-driven convective transport
//   increases capture efficiency over diffusion-limited methods
//   (accounts for slightly higher RNA yield at 100 uL blood
//   volume vs. conventional method; ref Model & Omann 1995).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bridgingDroplet2 = mix bridgingDroplet1 with devicePrime for 5s
heat bridgingDroplet2 at 25c for 60s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: World-to-DMF transition -- Pass 3 of 3.
//   Final pass ensures maximum bead-bound RNA capture from
//   the reaction mixture before supernatant is sent to waste.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bridgingDroplet3 = mix bridgingDroplet2 with devicePrime for 5s
heat bridgingDroplet3 at 25c for 60s

// Send lysate supernatant to Waste Chamber. Beads remain
// immobilised on Bridging Electrode surface via magnet.
// beadCapture represents the bead pellet on the Bridging
// Electrode after all three passes; it is the live handle
// threaded through each subsequent wash/elution step.
// NOTE: bridgingDroplet1, bridgingDroplet2, and bridgingDroplet3
// are all consumed as inputs to their respective successor mix
// calls and must not be read again after those assignments.
// The fresh beadCapture variable avoids the stale-use bug
// that caused wash1Mix and wash2Mix to re-read already-consumed
// bridgingDroplet2[0] and bridgingDroplet1[0] in the original IR.
beadCapture      = mix bridgingDroplet3 with devicePrime for 5s
heat beadCapture at 25c for 10s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Wash 1 -- deliver Wash 1 Solution to Bridging Electrode.
//   Purification Module actuates Wash 1 droplet (15 uL) onto
//   the Bridging Electrode via electrowetting (adjacent electrode
//   pads energised sequentially). Magnet disengaged to release
//   beads into Wash 1 droplet. Bolus shuttled between Transfer
//   Tubes via Bridging Droplet for thorough bead washing.
//   Then magnet re-engaged to immobilise beads; fluid sent to
//   Waste Chamber.
//   Wash 1 composition: per MagMAX-Blood kit instructions.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Disengage magnet (model: heat at 25 C for resuspension time).
heat wash1Reservoir at 25c for 10s

wash1Mix         = mix wash1Reservoir with beadCapture for 30s
heat wash1Mix at 25c for 30s

// Re-engage magnet to recover beads; send wash fluid to waste.
// wash1Beads carries the bead pellet forward to Wash 2.
wash1Beads       = mix wash1Mix with devicePrime for 5s
dispose wash1Mix

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Wash 2 -- deliver Wash 2 Solution to Bridging Electrode.
//   Identical sequence to Wash 1: magnet disengage -> resuspend
//   -> shuttle/mix -> magnet engage -> send supernatant to waste.
//   Wash 2 removes residual chaotropic salts and ethanol
//   carried over from Wash 1, ensuring clean RNA eluate.
//   Wash 2 composition: per MagMAX-Blood kit instructions.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat wash2Reservoir at 25c for 10s

wash2Mix         = mix wash2Reservoir with wash1Beads for 30s
heat wash2Mix at 25c for 30s

// wash2Beads carries the bead pellet forward to Elution.
wash2Beads       = mix wash2Mix with devicePrime for 5s
dispose wash2Mix

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Elution -- deliver Elution Buffer to Bridging Electrode.
//   Elution Buffer (10 uL, +0.1% Pluronic F127) actuated onto
//   Bridging Electrode. Magnet disengaged; beads resuspended.
//   Bead-RNA complex incubated in Elution Buffer at room
//   temperature to release RNA from silica bead surface.
//   Magnet re-engaged to immobilise beads; RNA-containing
//   eluate driven to a recovery site within the Purification
//   Module for collection by pipetting and off-device analyses.
//   Eluate volume: 10 uL (5- to 15-fold concentration vs.
//   conventional 30 uL elution; 12-fold reagent reduction).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat elutionReservoir at 25c for 10s

elutionMix       = mix elutionReservoir with wash2Beads for 30s

// Incubate to maximise RNA elution from bead surface.
heat elutionMix at 25c for 120s

// Re-engage magnet; drive RNA-containing eluate to recovery site.
heat elutionMix at 25c for 30s

// elutionMix is now the RNA eluate at the recovery site.
// Total processing time: ~20 min (vs. 45 min conventional).

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: RNA yield QC.
//   rnaYieldDetection module abstracts NanoDrop 2000 UV-vis
//   spectrophotometry (A260) or Qubit 2.0 fluorometry
//   (off-device). Acceptable yield range: per literature
//   (Robison et al. 2009; Menke et al. 2012) for 50 uL blood.
//   World-to-DMF system gives comparable yield to conventional
//   preparations from 25 and 50 uL blood (p >= 0.16).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

yieldSignal      = detect rnaYieldDetection on elutionMix for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: RNA yield QC gate
// Pass (BB2): yieldSignal > 0 -> acceptable RNA yield detected.
// Fail (BB3): yieldSignal <= 0 -> insufficient yield.
// NOTE: elutionMix must NOT be disposed here. It must remain
// live across the join (BB4) so the purity detect in BB4 can
// read it. Disposing elutionMix inside either branch causes the
// compiler to generate a phantom SSA version (elutionMix2[0])
// at the purity detect site -- an unassigned variable. The
// yield gate therefore only records the signal outcome; actual
// disposal of elutionMix happens in BB5/BB6 after purity QC.
// NOTE: 'drain' is not emitted by this compiler; the IR always
// produces dispose regardless. Both branches use dispose here
// to keep source and IR semantically consistent.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (yieldJoin).
// ============================================================

if (yieldSignal > 0) {
    dispose yieldSignal
} else {
    dispose yieldSignal
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: yieldJoin
// elutionMix is still live here. Control falls into purity QC.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 11: RNA purity QC.
//   rnaPurityDetection module abstracts A260/A280 ratio
//   measurement (NanoDrop 2000, off-device). A260/A280 >= 1.8
//   indicates acceptable RNA purity free of protein
//   contamination. RNA integrity (RIN) measured on 2100
//   Bioanalyzer with RNA 6000 Pico Chip (off-device).
//   World-to-DMF preparations show RIN and A260/A280 values
//   comparable to conventional preparations (Table S-1, paper).
//   Downstream applications supported: qRT-PCR (B2M, GAPDH,
//   PPIB housekeeping genes) and RNA-Seq (Illumina MiSeq,
//   50 bp single-end, ~99% mapping to hg19, R^2 ~0.85 vs
//   conventional).
//   NOTE: puritySignal is a virtual droplet representing the
//   QC readout, consistent with BioScript conventions for
//   off-device detection operations.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

puritySignal     = detect rnaPurityDetection on elutionMix for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: RNA purity QC gate
// Pass (BB5): puritySignal > 0 -> A260/A280 acceptable (>= 1.8);
//             RNA preparation complete and verified. Dispose eluate.
// Fail (BB6): puritySignal <= 0 -> low purity (salt or protein
//             carryover). Dispose eluate.
// NOTE: 'drain' is not emitted by this compiler; both pass and
// fail branches use dispose so source matches IR output exactly.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (puritySignal > 0) {
    dispose elutionMix
} else {
    dispose elutionMix
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose purity QC signal droplet. Experiment ends.
// RNA eluate has been collected off-device for downstream
// qRT-PCR or RNA-Seq analysis.
// ============================================================

dispose puritySignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]
